findings.message:
In ec_pair_dec(), p_data[0]/p_data[1] are initially set to point at the base of the fixed-size local arrays data_pair[0]/data_pair[1] (each int16_t[28]) and are then passed by reference (int16_t *data[2] decays to int16_t **) into huff_decode(). Inside huff_decode(), for the 2D 'time_pair' coding path, the code executes 'data[0] += 1; data[1] += 1;' to skip over an already-decoded first sample. Because the pointer array itself is passed by reference, this mutates the caller's p_data[0]/p_data[1] pointer values, advancing them one int16_t element past the start of data_pair[0]/data_pair[1].

After huff_decode() returns, the code unconditionally uses p_data[0] and p_data[1] (which may now be pointing one element into the array, not at its base) as both the input (data_msb) and output (data) buffer for attach_lsb(), iterating 'nb_bands' elements:

    attach_lsb(gb, p_data[0], quant_offset, attach_lsb_flag, nb_bands, p_data[0]);
    if (pair)
        attach_lsb(gb, p_data[1], quant_offset, attach_lsb_flag, nb_bands, p_data[1]);

When p_data[0]/p_data[1] have been advanced by 1 element and nb_bands equals the full array capacity (up to 28, matching MPS_MAX_PARAM_BANDS-derived sizes), attach_lsb's internal loop 'for (i = 0; i < nb_val; i++) data[i] = ...' writes/reads at index nb_bands-1 relative to the advanced base pointer, i.e. one element past the end of the 28-element data_pair[]/data_diff[] stack arrays. This results in an out-of-bounds stack read (data_msb[i]) and out-of-bounds stack write (data[i]) that can corrupt adjacent stack variables, triggerable via a crafted USAC/MPS212 bitstream (reported as poc.wav).

